The processor must enforce strict in-order delivery while remaining idempotent. Applied identifiers and buffered identifiers are tracked separately, and future sequence numbers are held in a map keyed by `seq`. An incoming event is ignored when its `id` was already applied or buffered, when its `seq` is at or below `lastSeq`, or when the same `seq` is already buffered. If the event is exactly the next expected sequence (`lastSeq+1`) it is applied immediately and then the buffer is drained consecutively, moving each drained identifier from the buffered set to the applied set and advancing `lastSeq`. Otherwise the event is stored in the buffer. Applying is performed before mutating state so a throwing `apply` callback does not corrupt `lastSeq` or the buffer, and `pendingCount` simply reflects the buffer size.

```mjs
// Applies stream events to some state via the `apply` callback.
// Event shape: { id: string, seq: number, type: string, payload?: any }

export class EventProcessor {
  /**
   * @param {(event: object) => void} apply
   * @param {{ startSeq?: number }} [opts]
   */
  constructor(apply, { startSeq = 1 } = {}) {
    this.apply = apply;
    this.startSeq = startSeq;
    this.lastSeq = startSeq - 1;

    // internal state for ordering & idempotency
    this._buffer = new Map(); // seq -> event
    this._bufferedIds = new Set(); // ids currently buffered
    this._appliedIds = new Set(); // ids already applied
  }

  /** Number of events received but not yet applied. */
  get pendingCount() {
    return this._buffer.size;
  }

  /**
   * Handle one delivered event.
   * @returns {object[]} events applied as a result of this call, in order
   */
  process(event) {
    // Defensive: ignore malformed events
    if (!event || typeof event.id !== 'string' || typeof event.seq !== 'number') {
      return [];
    }

